  "result": "The gate passed `OK` (8 witnesses) on run 3 of 6. After that run I changed only a comment in the witness file's header, so the harness's own re-run should give the same result.\n\n**Rule** (`LVMC_11_30_060_B.lean`):\n- **When it applies:** the subject is a pedestrian (under the `DEF-NRS-484A-165::pedestrian` definition) who is on a roadway and walking across it. \"Across\" is tested against the roadway's own direction or a lane the pedestrian is in. A pedestrian tunnel or overpass must be within `p.grade_separation_reach` of their body, and no \"pedestrians prohibited\" sign may govern that roadway.\n- **What it requires:** for every vehicle on a roadway chosen by those same tests, the pedestrian must not touch it. They also must not be moving while the projected closest approach of the two centres comes within `p.conflict_horizon` and below `p.conflict_centre_clearance`.\n- **Choices:** I declared no narrowings. The only choices are writing \"a lane or the roadway\" as an either/or, and reading \"vehicles upon the roadway\" as vehicles on any roadway that passes the same tests. Both follow the node's own wording.\n\n**Witnesses:** the four planned scenes, plus four branch scenes I added so that every part of the formula affects at least one result:\n- **`branch_1`:** the roadway has no direction, so the crossing is detected through a lane.\n- **`branch_2`:** a \"pedestrians prohibited\" sign is present, so the rule doesn't apply.\n- **`branch_3`:** a car on the roadway is driving away, so the pedestrian complies.\n- **`branch_4`:** the conflicting car is on a neighbouring roadway that has a \"pedestrians prohibited\" sign, so it doesn't count and the pedestrian complies.\n\n**Gate runs:**\n1. **`UNUSED_CLAIM`:** I ran the gate once on the untouched skeleton as a baseline.\n2. **`VACUOUS`:** in `forbid`, the car and pedestrian touched at the third instant, which hid the speed and closest-approach checks. The sign checks inside the obligation also had no scene that decided them. I cut `forbid` to two instants, so the violation is the projected conflict rather than contact, and added `branch_4`.\n3. **`OK`.**\n\nI didn't find anything wrong with the node or the concepts it calls. One read-only search of Core (a `grep` to check how one atom takes its arguments) was blocked because only the gate command may run here. I wrote that argument from the surface's argument table instead, and the gate accepted it.\n\nThe full report is in `task_result.md` in the working directory.",
